# Task 156-010-fix — Spawn-Time File Lock Hardening Plan

## Baseline state (before fix)
- src/core/file-lock.ts already exports SpawnLockInfo / SpawnLockError /
  acquireSpawnLock / releaseSpawnLock / acquireSpawnLocks (with rollback) /
  releaseSpawnLocks / releaseAllSpawnLocks. 18-case test suite at
  tests/core/file-lock-runtime.test.ts is green (verified locally).
- DockerSpawnBackend.spawn() calls acquireSpawnTimeLocks(); on docker
  image absence it releases. monitorContainer + kill() release on exit.

## Identified gap (the actual bug behind the NO_GO)
DockerSpawnBackend.spawn() acquires spawn locks early, then runs
`spawnSync('docker', ...)` to launch the container. When that returns a
non-zero status (engine refuses, image broken, volume mount error, etc.)
the current code writes a `.timeout` marker and `return`s — without ever
releasing the spawn locks. Result: every subsequent retry for the same
task fails with `SpawnLockError` from the leftover lock owned by the
SAME taskId. Idempotency saves us in practice for the same taskId, but
fix-worker spawns use a new id (`<taskId>-fix`) and would NOT be the
owner — turning a transient docker failure into a permanent block on
the original file scope.

A similar leak exists between `acquireSpawnTimeLocks` and the docker
image check / prompt write / script write — any throw there would leave
locks dangling.

## Fix
1. src/orchestra/spawn-backend-docker.ts
   - Wrap the whole post-lock body of `spawn()` in a try/catch.
   - On any throw OR on the non-zero docker-run early-return path, call
     `releaseAllSpawnLocks(dir, taskId)` first, then rethrow / return.
   - Keep the existing image-not-found release path (now redundant
     thanks to the wrapper, but explicit is fine).

2. tests/core/file-lock-runtime.test.ts
   - Add a regression test that simulates a leak-prone caller pattern:
     acquire spawn locks for [a,b], simulate failure, call
     releaseAllSpawnLocks, then confirm a different task can claim the
     same files. (No docker dependency — we're hardening the contract
     consumed by the docker backend.)

## Out of scope
- tmux / subprocess backend wiring — DIRECTIVES Task 10 explicitly
  targets only the docker backend.
- ADR drafts (other Sprint 156 tasks).
- Any build invocation (ANCHOR: build = Alperen).

## Verification
- `npx tsc --noEmit` → 0 errors.
- `npx vitest run tests/core/file-lock-runtime.test.ts tests/core/file-lock.test.ts`
  → green.

## Files
- src/orchestra/spawn-backend-docker.ts (defensive try/catch wrapper)
- tests/core/file-lock-runtime.test.ts (regression test)
- src/core/file-lock.ts (no functional change — leave as-is)
